'' A SHARED INITIALISER INSIDE A NAMESPACE WAS RUN BY NOBODY.
''
'' N_NAMESPACE and N_EXTERN_BLOCK both sat in the statement dispatch's DECLARATIONS
'' list, on the grounds that the MODULE WALK handles them. WalkTop lowers N_PROC and
'' nothing else, so a shared variable nested in either got its storage and NO
'' initialising store at all:
''
''     namespace o : dim shared as long a = 7 : end namespace
''     print o.a          fbc: 7      tiko: 0
''
'' A WRONG VALUE, silently, in a program that compiles, verifies and links -- and the
'' same for 'extern "C"', whose own note in lower.bas claimed it was handled. Measured,
'' it was not. A NESTED namespace is here too, which is why the walk recurses rather
'' than looking one level down.
''
'' Neither block may hold anything executable -- fbc's error 96 -- so only the
'' declarations are descended into and every other child stays the module walk's.
''
'' Found by build/goldenrun.sh: two MIR goldens held the missing initialiser, and both
'' of those tests print the variable, so RUNNING them is what said so.
''
'' Differenced against fbc: 7 / 7 / 1.5 / 9 / 5.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/namespace-shared-init.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered

'' EVERY ONE OF THEM CARRIES ITS VALUE, including the one at module scope that always
'' did -- so a regression that dropped the lot would still fail here.
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 top = 7
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 a = 7
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 deep = 9
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 ex = 5
'' GOLDEN: expected/namespace-shared-init.out
